WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.409 --> 00:00:07.250
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:21.439
Welcome back to Superstars in History, the talk show featuring famous people from the past.

3
00:00:22.379 --> 00:00:27.943
Today's guest is one of the most beloved and glamorous movie stars of all time.

4
00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:38.031
She's an EGOT, a rare individual who's won every major American entertainment award: Emmy, Grammy, Oscar, and Tony.

5
00:00:39.292 --> 00:00:44.396
And she's equally famous for her humanitarian work on behalf of the world's children.

6
00:00:44.976 --> 00:00:49.199
Straight from history, please welcome Audrey Hepburn!

7
00:01:06.156 --> 00:01:07.117
Hello, Faye.

8
00:01:07.997 --> 00:01:10.339
I'm so excited to finally meet you!

9
00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:12.100
I'm such a huge fan.

10
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.024
I know you were born in Belgium in 1929, but I don't know anything about your family.

11
00:01:19.844 --> 00:01:24.887
My mother was my main influence because my father left us when I was six.

12
00:01:25.548 --> 00:01:31.511
She was a Dutch baroness and had wanted to study opera, but her family disapproved of a career

13
00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:32.372
on the stage.

14
00:01:33.172 --> 00:01:37.613
So she put all her creative energy into encouraging my interest in ballet.

15
00:01:38.394 --> 00:01:43.655
I had two half brothers, Alexander and Ian, from my mother's first marriage.

16
00:01:44.055 --> 00:01:47.196
They were much older than I, but we were very close.

17
00:01:47.936 --> 00:01:52.077
You grew up to be an actress—you must have been an outgoing child.

18
00:01:52.958 --> 00:01:55.598
On the contrary, I was quite shy.

19
00:01:56.399 --> 00:02:02.016
My mother sent me away to boarding school in England when I was only five years old, thinking

20
00:02:02.056 --> 00:02:03.678
it would make me more outgoing.

21
00:02:04.859 --> 00:02:11.204
When World War II started in 1939, she took me to the Netherlands, where her family lived.

22
00:02:11.724 --> 00:02:17.489
She thought we'd be safer there because Great Britain and Nazi Germany were at war, and the Netherlands

23
00:02:17.549 --> 00:02:18.930
was a neutral country.

24
00:02:19.911 --> 00:02:23.994
But we were still in danger because we lived near the German border.

25
00:02:24.715 --> 00:02:30.740
My mother warned me never to speak English in public for fear that German soldiers might overhear me

26
00:02:31.188 --> 00:02:32.409
and think I was British.

27
00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:37.874
I learned in history class that being neutral didn't help the Netherlands.

28
00:02:38.715 --> 00:02:43.219
The Germans occupied the country for five years, starting in 1940.

29
00:02:43.739 --> 00:02:45.380
That sounds so frightening!

30
00:02:46.281 --> 00:02:46.842
It was!

31
00:02:47.442 --> 00:02:53.407
My dear uncle was executed by the Nazis; my brother Ian was forced to work in a German

32
00:02:53.447 --> 00:02:58.492
munitions factory; and Alexander went into hiding to avoid the same fate.

33
00:02:59.204 --> 00:03:02.967
I was among a group of girls rounded up to work in German kitchens.

34
00:03:04.389 --> 00:03:07.231
I managed to escape, but that's another story.

35
00:03:08.372 --> 00:03:13.276
Is it true that you secretly worked against the Nazis as part of the Dutch Resistance?

36
00:03:14.497 --> 00:03:17.400
Oh, my role has been exaggerated over the years!

37
00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.623
Keep in mind that I was only 11 when the occupation started.

38
00:03:22.084 --> 00:03:26.988
I delivered a few messages and danced in clandestine recitals to benefit the Resistance.

39
00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.762
By the way, the audience never applauded for fear that the Nazis might discover us!

40
00:03:33.603 --> 00:03:37.464
I've heard that many in the Netherlands were starving during the war.

41
00:03:38.545 --> 00:03:44.327
Sometimes my family was so desperate for food that we made flour out of tulip bulbs!

42
00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:51.230
When the war ended in 1945, I was in poor health and suffering from malnutrition.

43
00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:55.732
A United Nations relief agency gave us food and clothing.

44
00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:01.624
I was so eager for a sweet treat that I drank a whole can of condensed milk at

45
00:04:01.784 --> 00:04:03.745
once! Oh my!

46
00:04:04.166 --> 00:04:06.267
What happened to your family after the war?

47
00:04:07.368 --> 00:04:10.730
Amazingly both my brothers returned safely to us.

48
00:04:11.891 --> 00:04:14.193
After that I returned to dancing.

49
00:04:14.793 --> 00:04:19.817
My mother and I soon moved to Amsterdam so I could take ballet lessons.

50
00:04:19.937 --> 00:04:24.160
In 1948 we moved to London, again for my dancing.

51
00:04:25.396 --> 00:04:26.877
How did you start acting?

52
00:04:28.158 --> 00:04:33.962
When my teacher told me I wasn't talented enough to be a professional ballerina, I joined the chorus

53
00:04:34.022 --> 00:04:35.663
line of a London musical.

54
00:04:36.964 --> 00:04:41.327
I later had a few bit parts in British movies and the lead in a French film.

55
00:04:42.588 --> 00:04:46.971
This might be a good time to talk about your influence on fashion and beauty.

56
00:04:47.452 --> 00:04:50.874
You certainly looked different from most actresses in the 1950s.

57
00:04:50.894 --> 00:04:55.750
I was thin and had short hair and thick eyebrows.

58
00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:02.275
While most women wore dresses and heels, I preferred pants and flat shoes because I was tall.

59
00:05:03.095 --> 00:05:08.079
I also looked best in a limited range of colors: black, white, and beige.

60
00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:14.764
I didn't consider myself pretty, so I was surprised when women wanted "the Audrey Hepburn look."

61
00:05:15.485 --> 00:05:17.646
And women still do today!

62
00:05:18.747 --> 00:05:19.908
Let's get back to your career.

63
00:05:20.412 --> 00:05:23.595
You came to the United States in 1951.

64
00:05:24.476 --> 00:05:27.939
Yes, I had the lead role in the Broadway play Gigi.

65
00:05:28.839 --> 00:05:36.266
My first Hollywood movie was Roman Holiday, which won me an Academy Award—or Oscar—in 1954.

66
00:05:37.087 --> 00:05:40.990
That same year I also earned a Tony Award for the play Ondine.

67
00:05:43.592 --> 00:05:46.755
Suddenly you were an international star at age 24!

68
00:05:48.492 --> 00:05:54.937
You're probably most famous for playing the lead in the movie Breakfast at Tiffany's, but my favorite is

69
00:05:55.097 --> 00:05:56.118
My Fair Lady.

70
00:05:56.899 --> 00:06:00.842
I'm glad you like it, but that movie was a difficult project for me.

71
00:06:01.503 --> 00:06:06.807
Julie Andrews played the lead role on Broadway, so many people thought she should have gotten the movie

72
00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:07.868
role instead of me.

73
00:06:08.748 --> 00:06:12.471
To prove them wrong, I worked very hard to master the songs.

74
00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:18.016
I was terribly disappointed when another woman was later hired to dub my singing.

75
00:06:19.617 --> 00:06:19.857
Oh no!

76
00:06:21.018 --> 00:06:24.901
Still, you were nominated for five Oscars during your career.

77
00:06:25.561 --> 00:06:26.862
Was that role one of them?

78
00:06:27.583 --> 00:06:32.627
No. Ironically Julie Andrews won that year for her role in Mary Poppins!

79
00:06:33.508 --> 00:06:37.031
You had pretty much stopped making movies by the late 1960s.

80
00:06:37.771 --> 00:06:40.574
Yes, I didn't like the parts I was being offered.

81
00:06:41.194 --> 00:06:46.979
By then I'd also started a family and wanted to devote more time to my two sons and

82
00:06:47.059 --> 00:06:48.140
home in Switzerland.

83
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:55.265
Yet you came out of retirement in the 1980s to serve as a goodwill ambassador for UNICEF, the

84
00:06:55.325 --> 00:06:58.648
United Nations agency that helps children and families.

85
00:06:59.668 --> 00:07:04.852
This work was so important to me because a forerunner of UNICEF had given me aid at the

86
00:07:05.012 --> 00:07:06.173
end of World War II.

87
00:07:07.134 --> 00:07:12.218
I knew what it was like to starve during wartime, and I'd always loved children.

88
00:07:13.119 --> 00:07:19.245
Starting in 1988 I traveled the world to raise money and awareness for children in need of food

89
00:07:19.405 --> 00:07:20.346
and medical care.

90
00:07:21.806 --> 00:07:27.347
You've certainly led an interesting life, but I think it's time we let you return to history.

91
00:07:28.388 --> 00:07:29.868
Thank you, Audrey Hepburn!

92
00:07:30.508 --> 00:07:31.348
Thank you, Faye!

93
00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:44.032
In December 1992 Audrey Hepburn received the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the highest civilian award in the United

94
00:07:44.092 --> 00:07:47.726
States. She died on January 20, 1993.

95
00:07:47.966 --> 00:07:54.611
The next day the first episode of her TV series, Gardens of the World, premiered.

96
00:07:55.412 --> 00:08:01.136
She was posthumously awarded an Emmy for that series, as well as a Grammy for an album of

97
00:08:01.296 --> 00:08:02.237
children's tales.

98
00:08:02.937 --> 00:08:06.279
And that's how she became one of the first EGOTs!

99
00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:07.961
That's all for today.

100
00:08:08.481 --> 00:08:11.723
See you next time on Superstars in History.
